    <item>
      <title>Rolling Release Distros Superior To LTS Distros In The Patch-Heavy GenAI Era?</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Rolling-Releases-GenAI-Era</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Rolling-Releases-GenAI-Era</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 15:27:12 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="OPERATING SYSTEMS" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/operatingsystems.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; As some interesting food for thought and weekend forum discussions, this week at the Linux Plumbers Conference in Prague, Qualcomm engineer Khem Raj questioned the relevance of Linux Long Term Support (LTS) distributions in the era of generative AI with the constantly heavy patch flow and bug reports. Khem Raj argued the benefits of rolling release distributions in the GenAI era and the benefits it provides staying up-to-date with mainline. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With AI coding agents continue to increase the rate of patch flow to the Linux kernel and surrounding open-source projects, it continues to apply not only a large burden on the open-source maintainers but also the distribution vendors needing to deal with back-porting patches to their given kernel trees and the like. All of this patch burden is creating bottlenecks from CI/CD to release management. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=lts_woes_1_med" alt="Linux LTS challenges" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Raj argued the benefits of rolling release distributions with less of a need for back-porting all these patches, maintaining the smaller divergence to upstream, faster security fixes, and more. Granted, the rolling release model presents challenges around certification and qualification and other challenges. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=lts_woes_2_med" alt="Linux LTS maintenance burden" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The presentation does present some valid points about the increased burden downstream for Linux distribution vendors maintaining LTS releases with the increased patch back-porting burden and testing. It will be interesting to see how LTS distributions evolve over time and if rolling releases become more palatable to large enterprise organizations. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=lts_woes_3_med" alt="Linux LTS vs. rolling release distros" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those wanting to go through all of the material can do so via the &lt;a href="https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2586/"&gt;LPC presentation page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Many Laptop &amp; Gaming Handheld Support Improvements Coming For Linux 7.4</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-Laptops-Handhelds</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-Laptops-Handhelds</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 13:40:51 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Many x86 platform driver improvements have been queuing ahead of the upcoming Linux 7.4 merge window to offer a variety of laptop and gaming handheld device improvements with this next kernel version. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;A lot of device quirks and other support improvements have recently been accumulating within the x86 platform driver's &lt;a href="https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/pdx86/platform-drivers-x86.git/log/?h=for-next"&gt;for-next Git branch&lt;/a&gt;. Some of these improvements on deck for Linux 7.4 include: &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The ASUS-NB-WMI driver is adding a tablet mode quirk for the ASUS ROG Flow X13 (GV302X*) 2023 models so when converting from laptop to/from tablet mode will be properly handled on Linux. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The ASUS Armoury driver is seeing a new fallback for enabling/disabling the discrete GPU on newer ASUS laptops like the ProArt laptops (e.g. H7606 series). &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The MSI EC driver adds MSI Katana 15 B13VFK on its newer firmware for being able to handle battery charge threshold controls. The MSI GL65 Leopard 9SCXK also sees similar support. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The HP WMI driver is adding thermal profile support for the HP OMEN 17-ck1xxx (board 8A17). &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The HP Victus 15-fb3xxx (board ID: 8DD0) is also newly supported by the HP WMI driver. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The Uniwill laptop driver is enabling keyboard backlight and lightbar control support for TUXEDO Computers laptops. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The Acer WMI driver adds support for the Acer Nitro ANV15-51 for thermal profile handling, hardware monitoring, and fan control. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The OXPEC driver for OneXPlayer hardware adds a quirk for the new Intel-powered OneXPlayer 3 to enjoy battery bypass, capacity limits, fan curves, fan tachometer, turbo button override, and related functionality. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The AYANEO EC driver adds battery charge control support for the AYANEO 2S. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Linux 7.4 merge window opens later this month while the stable v7.4 kernel should be out around the end of December.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KNOD Progressing For Linux In-Kernel GPU Offloading For Network Packet Processing</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/KNOD-2026-GPU-Network-Process</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/KNOD-2026-GPU-Network-Process</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:25:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX NETWORKING" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxnetworking.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; A while back we covered &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KNOD-Network-Offload-AMD-GPUs"&gt;KNOD as an experimental solution for in-kernel network offloading&lt;/a&gt; to AMD GPUs. The open-source KNOD project allows leveraging GPUs to handle packet processing in a very performant and energy efficient manner without needing any specialized hardware. KNOD has continued and a development update was shared this week at the Linux Plumbers Conference in Prague. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;KNOD continues showing a lot of interesting potential for exploiting GPUs as a kernel-managed packet-processing accelerator for networking.  KNOD will JIT compile an XDP program into GPU machine code, the network adapter will place packets directly into GPU-accessible memory, and the GPU will process them. RX IPsec abilities were recently added beyond the original XDP programs. KNOD has proven performant with getting up to 70 million packets per second for XDP workloads and 80 Gbit/s for RX IPsec on consumer GPU hardware. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=knod_lpc_1" alt="KNOD overview" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The developers behind KNOD find it to be very cost effective as well as being more power efficient than just using the CPU for packet processing. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=knod_lpc_2" alt="KNOD is cheap" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=knod_lpc_3" alt="KNOD power consumption" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;While AMD GPUs have been their main target to date, it was mentioned at LPC 2026 they are going to explore Intel Arc graphics support too as well as seeing that KNOD will work with the likes of Broadcom and Intel NICs too. AMD integrated graphics will also be explored rather than just discrete GPUs. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=knod_lpc_4" alt="KNOD TODO items" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those wishing to learn more about the interesting KNOD concept can do so via &lt;a href="https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2462/"&gt;the LPC presentation assets&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux Networking Diverts 50+ Fixes To Next Kernel, Still Bombarded With AI Fueled Churn</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-rc7-Networking</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-rc7-Networking</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:11:36 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX NETWORKING" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxnetworking.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Ahead of the Linux 7.3-rc7 kernel due for release on Sunday, this week's batch of networking fixes were submitted and merged. The vast networking subsystem of wired and wireless drivers plus the various other networking features continues to see a lot of activity due to AI-driven bug reports and patches.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Last week &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Networking-Linux-7.3-rc6"&gt;some networking fixes began to be held off until Linux 7.4&lt;/a&gt; due to getting late in the Linux 7.3 cycle and still seeing a lot of patch activity due to the AI/LLM-driven world. So the fixes not particularly pressing began to be queued in net-next rather than rushing them into Linux 7.3. The Linux networking maintainers already communicated before that they've been &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-Networking"&gt;"completely overwhelmed" by all the AI activity&lt;/a&gt;. They've also been facing &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-WiFi-Strict-AI-Slop-Patch"&gt;challenges due to AI slop patches&lt;/a&gt;.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=cutting_ethernet_sml" alt="scissors to Ethernet" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;In this week's networking pull request, it's noted they've now diverted over 50 patches to net-next for Linux 7.4 rather than squeezing it into Linux 7.3. And yet it's still another big pull request for what was submitted this week for 7.3-rc7. Jakub Kicinski commented in the &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/179152126269.566079.1895099510348236501.pr-tracker-bot@kernel.org/T/#t"&gt;pull request&lt;/a&gt;:  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Pretty sure you won't be impressed by the size of this PR but this is after we shaved off 50 fixes to net-next and asked sub-trees to significantly trim their PRs. Situation is what it is."&lt;/blockquote&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;There are various Ethernet driver regression fixes, Mediatek MT76 WiFi driver fixes, and various other "LLM-ish" fixes to find with Linux 7.3-rc7 in tomorrow's release.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KDE Plasma 6.8 Ready For Release Next Week, Sees Last Minute NVIDIA Fix</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Next-Week</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Next-Week</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:00:51 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="KDE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/kde.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; KDE Plasma 6.8 is gearing up for its official debut next week in coinciding with the 30th anniversary since the KDE project began. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This Week in Plasma is out with its latest issue to summarize all the interesting developments -- fixes -- in the lead-up to next week's stable debut. Among the last minute changes to Plasma 6.8 are improved keyboard navigation for arrow keys in KRunner. Discover's functionality to automatically apply updates now will always work correctly for Flatpak apps, and other minor fixes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 also saw a last minute fix for an issue that could cause lag and stuttering when tiling windows with certain NVIDIA GPUs using the proprietary driver stack. The fix was to use a single persistent OpenGL context for Qt to avoid re-creating the context every time a Qt Quick effect is opened, which ends up briefly blocking the main thread. The NVIDIA issue was &lt;a href="https://bugs.kde.org/show_bug.cgi?id=522424"&gt;reported in June&lt;/a&gt; and affecting all recent Plasma versions. The fix is also tied to &lt;a href="https://bugs.kde.org/show_bug.cgi?id=479250"&gt;this early 2024 bug report&lt;/a&gt; too. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=cachyos-desktops-july-2026&amp;image=cachyos_desktops_2_med" alt="NVIDIA on KDE Plasma" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;KDE Frameworks 6.32 meanwhile fixed an issue with Qt based apps using blur where they could crash when a tooltip disappeared while the user is typing. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For Plasma 6.9 there are some settings adjustments. Plasma 6.9 is also making virtual desktop thumbnails within KWin's Overview effect more "harmonious and visually pleasing". Also for Plasma 6.9 is allowing the brightness level of backlit keyboards to be controlled that are connected after initial log-in. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the KDE Plasma changes ahead of next week's Plasma 6.9.0 stable debut can be found via &lt;a href="https://blogs.kde.org/2026/10/10/this-week-in-plasma-finishing-touches-on-6.8/"&gt;This Week in Plasma&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>SQLite 3.54 Released With Faster Performance, Drops Windows XP Support</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/SQLite-3.54-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/SQLite-3.54-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 00:41:04 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="PROGRAMMING" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/programming.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; SQLite 3.54 released today for this wildly popular embedded SQL database engine used by countless applications and other software. With SQLite 3.54, support for Microsoft Windows XP and older is finally removed. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;SQLite 3.54 finally does away with Windows XP support. Windows XP and earlier Windows versions are no longer supported, including Windows CE. Windows Vista and newer is still maintained. In the process, SQLite on Windows now has improved performance for Slim Reader/Write Locks and other enhancements to benefit the more modern Windows versions. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;SQLite 3.54 also ships with CLI prompt enhancements, new mode command options, query planner improvements, support for &lt;em&gt;O_TMPFILE&lt;/em&gt; on systems supporting it for temporary file generation, and more. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2022&amp;image=sqlite" alt="SQLite logo" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Overall, SQLite 3.54 is exciting for coming in roughly 4% faster according to their own benchmarks in the Speedtest performance benchmark on Linux x64. SQLite 3.54 is also around 7.5% faster than SQLite 3.51 from one year ago. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on the SQLite 3.54 embedded database library via &lt;a href="https://sqlite.org/releaselog/3_54_0.html"&gt;SQLite.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ubuntu 26.10 To Include Desktop Images For RISC-V</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ubuntu-26.10-RISC-V-Desktop</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ubuntu-26.10-RISC-V-Desktop</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:59:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="UBUNTU" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/ubuntu.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Ubuntu Linux ISOs for RISC-V 64-bit to date have just been the server/CLI version without any desktop environment pre-seeded. But with next week's Ubuntu 26.10 release, there will now be Ubuntu 26.10 RISC-V desktop ISOs for both Ubuntu proper and a Xubuntu minimal ISO. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Valentin Haudiquet announced the work on the Ubuntu 26.10 RISC-V desktop images that will be available with the official release on 15 October. There is the regular Ubuntu Desktop image for RISC-V as well as an unofficial Xubuntu minimal image with the lighter-weight Xfce desktop. Testing has been focused on the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/SpacemiT+K3"&gt;SpacemiT K3&lt;/a&gt; hardware that is RVA23 profile compliant. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=ubuntu_2610_riscv" alt="Ubuntu 26.10 RISC-V desktop" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For those testing in QEMU, the Xubuntu version is recommended to avoid a painfully slow experience: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"The Xubuntu variant exists for a practical reason: the XFCE-based minimal image is far more manageable under emulation (the GNOME desktop can take 30+ minutes to load under QEMU)."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on these RISC-V desktop images with Ubuntu 26.10 can be found via &lt;a href="https://discourse.ubuntu.com/t/announcing-ubuntu-desktop-images-for-risc-v/89110"&gt;Ubuntu Discourse&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Valve's Proton 11.0-2e Released To Fix EA App Issues</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Steam-Play-Proton-11.0-2e</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Steam-Play-Proton-11.0-2e</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:56:40 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="VALVE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/valve.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; CodeWeavers working with Valve today released Proton 11.0-2e as their downstream of Wine that powers Steam Play for enjoying Windows games on Linux. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Proton 11.0-2e release brings just a few changes pertaining to enhancing the EA App support under Steam Play. EA App is Electronic Arts' digital distribution / game management platform that replaced Origin. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=ea_app" alt="EA App" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The only listed changes of Proton 11.0-2e include: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"- Fixed EA App Launcher login window not being focused properly with Gamescope. &lt;br /&gt;- Fixed EA App titles not displaying the correct splash image."&lt;/blockquote&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Useful for those wanting to run EA App on the Steam Deck / Steam Machines, especially the Gamescope fix, and other Linux hardware. The release assets for Proton 11.0-2e can be found via &lt;a href="https://github.com/ValveSoftware/Proton/releases/tag/proton-11.0-2e"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Python 3.15 Released With Experimental JIT Compiler Running Faster</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Python-3.15-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Python-3.15-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 14:58:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="PROGRAMMING" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/programming.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Python 3.15 is out today as the newest annual feature release for the Python programming language.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Python's JIT compiler is still in experimental form but it's a lot faster now with Python 3.15.  The JIT compiler should be 7~8% geo mean faster on Linux x86-64 over Python's standard interpreter. On AArch64 macOS the JIT compiler is said to be 11~12% faster over the tail-calling interpreter.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Python 3.15 also introduces the sentinel built-in type, UTF-8 is the default encoding of Python 3.15, explicit lazy imports for faster Python script startup times, a new "frozendict" built-in type, and package startup configuration files.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=python_315_sml" alt="Python 3.15 release logo" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Python 3.15 also benefits from improved error messages, standard library enhancements, C API improvements. Python 3.15 also enables frame pointers by default for improved system-level observability.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on today's Python 3.15 release at &lt;a href="https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-3150/"&gt;Python.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AMD PerfOpt Boosting Prior-Gen Ryzen APU Performance For AI</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/review/amd-perfopt-prior-gen</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/review/amd-perfopt-prior-gen</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 14:38:27 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Last week I ran benchmarks of &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-PerfOpt-Linux-7.4"&gt;the new AMD PerfOpt feature being introduced in Linux 7.4&lt;/a&gt;. In those PerfOpt benchmarks on &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/amd-perfopt"&gt;current-gen AMD Ryzen AI APUs/SoCs the AI performance improved as much as 18~23%&lt;/a&gt;. Several Phoronix readers expressed interest in seeing how prior-gen AMD  Ryzen hardware benefited or not from PerfOpt, so here are some additional benchmarks.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=amd-perfopt-prior-gen&amp;image=amd_zen4_perfopt_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=amd-perfopt-prior-gen&amp;image=amd_zen4_perfopt_1_med" alt="AMD PerfOpt on Zen 4" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;This AMD IOMMU Performance Optimization feature AMD integrated graphics can bypass the IOMMU when directly accessing system memory to avoid the associated overhead. With Linux 7.4 this PerfOpt feature will be enabled by default when using Radeon integrated graphics.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=amd-perfopt-prior-gen&amp;image=amd_zen4_perfopt_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=amd-perfopt-prior-gen&amp;image=amd_zen4_perfopt_2_med" alt="AMD PerfOpt benchmarking on Ubuntu Linux" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For those curious how AMD PerfOpt works for prior-gen hardware, I pulled out an older Lenovo ThinkPad P14s Gen 4 laptop with the AMD Ryzen 7 PRO 7840U with Radeon 780M integrated graphics. The Radeon 780M is still fairly common and this Zen 4 laptop SoCs still fairly robust in 2026. As to be shown in these benchmarks, with PerfOpt there now is added performance potential for those still using this class of hardware.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="https://phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/result.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp Lemonade AMD Ryzen 7 PRO 7840U" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The benchmarks on this ThinkPad P14s G4 were done on Ubuntu 26.04 LTS while using a kernel build of the IOMMU next Git tree ahead of the Linux 7.4 merge window opening later this month. No other changes were made to the system during testing beyond toggling the PerfOpt functionality.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-debug-qwen3-14b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-debug, Model: Qwen3-14B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With this Ryzen 7 7840U laptop, with the forthcoming PerfOpt feature right away there was a visible improvement in lower latency for the time to first token within the Lemonade local AI server.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-short-qwen3-14b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-short, Model: Qwen3-14B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-debug-qwen35-4b-gguf.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-debug, Model: Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The token throughput was improving slightly with Lemonade and its Vulkan Llama.cpp back-end when having PerfOpt.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-debug-qwen35-4b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-debug, Model: Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-short-qwen35-4b-gguf.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-short, Model: Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-short-qwen35-4b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-short, Model: Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-debug-minicpm4-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-debug, Model: MiniCPM4-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-explain-qwen3-14b-gguf.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-explain, Model: Qwen3-14B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-explain-qwen3-14b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-explain, Model: Qwen3-14B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-short-minicpm4-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-short, Model: MiniCPM4-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-amd-rocm-code-debug-qwen35-4b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm, Scenario: code-debug, Model: Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-explain-qwen35-4b-gguf.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-explain, Model: Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-explain-qwen35-4b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-explain, Model: Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-explain-minicpm4-8b-gguf.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-explain, Model: MiniCPM4-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-explain-minicpm4-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-explain, Model: MiniCPM4-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-amd-rocm-code-explain-qwen35-4b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm, Scenario: code-explain, Model: Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-chat-long-output-qwen35-4b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: chat-long-output, Model: Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-amd-rocm-chat-long-output-qwen3-14b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm, Scenario: chat-long-output, Model: Qwen3-14B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-chat-long-output-minicpm4-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: chat-long-output, Model: MiniCPM4-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-debug-deepseek-qwen3-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-debug, Model: DeepSeek-Qwen3-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-short-deepseek-qwen3-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-short, Model: DeepSeek-Qwen3-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-amd-rocm-code-short-qwen3-coder-next-gguf.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm, Scenario: code-short, Model: Qwen3-Coder-Next-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-amd-rocm-code-short-qwen3-coder-next-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm, Scenario: code-short, Model: Qwen3-Coder-Next-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Both Vulkan and ROCm benefit from PerfOpt with Radeon iGPUs.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-code-explain-deepseek-qwen3-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: code-explain, Model: DeepSeek-Qwen3-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-amd-rocm-code-explain-qwen3-coder-next-gguf.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm, Scenario: code-explain, Model: Qwen3-Coder-Next-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-amd-rocm-code-explain-qwen3-coder-next-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm, Scenario: code-explain, Model: Qwen3-Coder-Next-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-amd-rocm-code-explain-deepseek-qwen3-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm, Scenario: code-explain, Model: DeepSeek-Qwen3-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-vulkan-chat-long-output-deepseek-qwen3-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Scenario: chat-long-output, Model: DeepSeek-Qwen3-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/lemonade-amd-rocm-chat-long-output-deepseek-qwen3-8b-gguf-1.svgz" alt="Lemonade benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm, Scenario: chat-long-output, Model: DeepSeek-Qwen3-8B-GGUF. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Across different models and scenarios tested with the Lemonade local AI server, the improvements were most noticeable for lower token latency but also some incremental throughput improvements. For those trying to make the most use of their hardware, PerfOpt will be enabled by default with Linux 7.4 to deliver this improvement.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/llamacpp-vulkan-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/llamacpp-vulkan-qwen35-9b-q8_0-pp2.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Model: Qwen3.5-9B-Q8_0, Test: Prompt Processing 2048. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/llamacpp-vulkan-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-pp2.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Prompt Processing 2048. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;When testing the Llama.cpp Vulkan performance directly, there were clear improvements in token generation and prompt processing benchmarks of PerfOpt on this AMD Zen 4 laptop.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/llamacpp-vulkan-glm-47-flash-iq4_xs-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Model: GLM-4.7-Flash-IQ4_XS, Test: Text Generation 128. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/llamacpp-vulkan-glm-47-flash-iq4_xs-pp2.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Model: GLM-4.7-Flash-IQ4_XS, Test: Prompt Processing 2048. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/llamacpp-lemonade-amd-ryzen-7-pro-7840u/llamacpp-vulkan-llama-31-tulu-3-8b-q8_0-pp2.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: Vulkan, Model: Llama-3.1-Tulu-3-8B-Q8_0, Test: Prompt Processing 2048. AMD PerfOpt was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;So for those wondering if prior-gen AMD hardware benefits from this AMD PerfOpt feature coming in Linux 7.4, yes it does. In testing on this Lenovo ThinkPad P14s G4 with the Ryzen 7 PRO 7840U with Radeon 780M graphics, 4~12% improvements were common across various AI benchmarks with the Radeon iGPU. Not bad for a mature platform and for a feature that will be enabled by default with this next Linux kernel version.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
